Protect an outbound video frame's RTP packets in one request, and send them in one call.

A process-wide SrtpProtector (rtc::SetSrtpProtectorFactory) takes over the outbound RTP of a connection once the DTLS
handshake gave its keys, and a Track hands the packets a media handler made of one frame to the transport together:
a crypto engine then protects a frame in one request instead of one per packet (NanoKVM's CryptoDMA chains AES-CTR and
SHA-1 per packet in one ioctl). Retransmissions go through the protector too, which keeps the rollover counters.
RTCP stays with libsrtp. Without a factory nothing changes.

The protected packets of a frame then go to libjuice together, which sends them with one sendmmsg system call on a direct UDP
pair (juice_send_batch; through a TURN relay one by one): a Wi-Fi driver gets them as a burst it can aggregate.

